---
description: Name tuple members with TypeAlias, NamedTuple, or dataclass
alwaysApply: true
---

# Named records, not positional tuples

Do not ship an anonymous `tuple[...]` when the members have names. Positions
are not names; two fields of the same type are indistinguishable and get
swapped in silence. That includes **dict keys**.

Use:

- **`TypeAlias`** when you are only renaming one existing type
- **`NamedTuple`** or a (frozen) **dataclass** when two or more members
  should be named — including lookup keys like campsite + unit type

```python
# BAD — slot key is an anonymous pair; return is four unlabelled dicts
why_by_slot: dict[tuple[str, int], list[dict[str, Any]]]
def _semantic_why_by_slot(...) -> tuple[dict, dict, dict, dict]:
    return why, missing, claims, rules

# GOOD
_WhyRows: TypeAlias = list[dict[str, Any]]

class _SlotKey(NamedTuple):
    campsite_id: str
    accommodation_type_id: int

@dataclass
class _SemanticWhy:
    why_by_slot: dict[_SlotKey, _WhyRows]
    reject_why_by_slot: dict[_SlotKey, _WhyRows]
    claims_by_site: dict[str, _WhyRows]
    rules_by_query: dict[str, dict[int, _WhyRows]]
```

Still fine: a throwaway pair you unpack in the next line and never type
(`ok, err = ...`). Not fine: a type, return value, or dict key whose
members you would name if you said them out loud.
